For CC in gcc-14 (14.2.0-19) and clang-19 (19.1.7):
  CC -std=gnu11 -O0 -g -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all
    -c SOURCE -o instrumented.o
  gcc-14 -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all instrumented.o
    -o instrumented
  valgrind --tool=memcheck --track-origins=yes --error-exitcode=97 instrumented
Both compile/link steps exit 0. Clang supplies its own instrumentation;
the final GCC driver supplies the available compatible libubsan runtime.
Both executions exit 97 with 9 errors from one context:
  Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised value(s)
    msum_i4 (20090113-1.c:43)
    main (20090113-1.c:59)
  Uninitialised value was created by a stack allocation
    main (20090113-1.c:53)

Controls: /bin/true under the same Memcheck exits 0 with zero errors.
Both unchanged originals compiled at O0 without UBSan also exit 0 with zero
Memcheck errors. This is expected: ordinary Memcheck transports unknown data
without reporting until it affects an observable decision. The UBSan overflow
check makes that use explicit; Memcheck, not UBSan alone, diagnoses its unknown
input. Neither original source nor expected output was modified. These are
positive dynamic initializedness observations from both independently compiled
original programs, not a claim that plain UBSan detects uninitialized memory.
Short raw diagnostic files: .build/floating-targeted/memcheck/
  uninit-gcc-14-{plain,ubsan}.memcheck
  uninit-clang-19-{plain,ubsan}.memcheck
The active frozen benchmark and its loaded disposition ledger are unchanged;
its result must be retained before any subsequent source-bound reclassification.
